From: HEVC enable on Pi 5
Subject: [PATCH] Probe /dev/video19 (rpi-hevc-dec) in addition to /dev/video10

The RPi-Distro patch hardcoded /dev/video10 (Pi 4 stateful H.264 decoder)
as the only candidate path in GetSupportedV4L2DecoderConfigs. On Pi 5 the
stateless HEVC decoder is exposed at /dev/video19 (rpi-hevc-dec, BCM2712).
Without probing it, chromium never advertises HEVC HW decode support and
falls back to software decode.

This patch keeps video10 for Pi 4 H.264 backward compat and adds video19
so chromium also enumerates HEVC profiles via the existing V4L2 stateless
H265 delegate path.

--- a/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_utils.cc
+++ b/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_utils.cc
@@ -587,8 +587,11 @@ std::optional<SupportedVideoDecoderConfi
 #elif BUILDFLAG(USE_V4L2_CODEC_RPI)
   // Pi stateful decoder here  current google code doesn't look like it can
   // cope with multiple devioces
-  constexpr char kVideoDevicePattern[] = "/dev/video10";
-  candidate_paths.push_back(kVideoDevicePattern);
+  // Pi 4 stateful H.264 / Pi 5 stateless HEVC decoders — probe both so that
+  // chromium reports HW decode support for all codecs the platform exposes.
+  candidate_paths.push_back("/dev/video10");
+  // Pi 5 BCM2712 rpi-hevc-dec (stateless HEVC).
+  candidate_paths.push_back("/dev/video19");
 #else
   constexpr char kVideoDevicePattern[] = "/dev/video";
   constexpr int kMaxDevices = 256;
